<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A minimal mathematical scaffold</p>
    <h2 id="t-compact-time">Put Infinite Time on a Finite Page Without Pretending to Reach It</h2>
    <p>Let ordinary time be \(t\in\mathbb R\), and compactify it only for description:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      u(t)=\frac{2}{\pi}\arctan t,
      \qquad -1&lt;u&lt;1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Following the existing TTI arrow toward the denser smallward infinity, one possible convention is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal I_{\rm large}:u=-1,
      \qquad
      \mathcal I_{\rm small}:u=+1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Reversing those labels would not change the non-synchronisation argument. What matters is that no
      finite \(t\) equals either boundary and no single finite event equals both. The reflection
      \(J(u)=-u\) pairs the endpoints mathematically, but a pairing is not a signal, a clock transport law or
      a physical identification. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">Compactification is established mathematics. Treating its two endpoints as physically
      real infinity-states is not. The map changes coordinates; it does not create a new cosmology.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What the key verb must mean</p>
    <h2 id="t-synchrony">An Instantaneous Match Is Not Full Synchronisation</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Two curves can cross once without becoming one clock. Full synchrony requires a
      relation that persists.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Phase coincidence</h3>
      <p>After choosing a lawful transport map to one comparison surface, two projected phases may satisfy
        \(\Delta\phi(t_*)=2\pi k\) at an isolated instant. That is an overlap. It neither identifies their
        origins nor guarantees another match.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Full synchronisation</h3>
      <p>Over a non-zero interval \(I\), complete phase locking would require both equal phase and equal rate:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Delta\phi(t)=2\pi k,
        \qquad
        \dot{\Delta\phi}(t)=0,
        \qquad t\in I.
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Operational comparison</h3>
      <p>Physical clock synchronisation also needs events, exchanged signals or a justified transport rule.
        A boundary at \(t=-\infty\) cannot send a round-trip timing pulse to \(t=+\infty\) through an ordinary
        finite causal protocol.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>The conditional result</h3>
      <p>If the endpoints are not identified, if comparison remains causal, and if no extra nonlocal law is
        supplied, then their endpoint states cannot be fully synchronised. The word <em>cannot</em> is earned
        only inside those assumptions. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What observers inside time might still see</p>
    <h2 id="t-overlap">Echoes, Periodic Meetings and Quasiperiodic Near-Meetings</h2>
    <p>Finite projections of the two boundary conditions could still appear in one interior history. If their
      effective frequencies are commensurate, projected phases may meet periodically. If the frequency ratio
      is irrational, they may return arbitrarily close without exactly repeating. A single alignment may also
      occur by initial condition or accident.</p>
    <p>Those are the same mathematical distinctions used in the
      <a href="/out-of-phase/#t-comparison">matter&ndash;antimatter phase essay</a>, but the subjects are
      different. Temporal boundary conditions are not particles, and a shared phase is not annihilation,
      baryogenesis, memory transfer or communication from infinity. The analogy may organise the question;
      it cannot supply the missing physics. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \text{finite overlap}
      \;\not\Rightarrow\;
      \text{common temporal origin}
      \;\not\Rightarrow\;
      \text{full synchronisation}.
    \]</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Topology does not settle the clock</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam">A Möbius Identification Would Be an Additional Law</h2>
    <p>An orientation-reversing mapping torus identifies spatial slices after a circuit. It does not reverse
      time or identify past and future infinity. TTI deliberately writes ordinary causal time separately as
      \(X=\mathbb R_t\times\mathcal M_f\).</p>
    <p>A stronger model could attempt to glue the temporal antipodes themselves. If it supplied a consistent
      causal structure, conserved transport and a phase law, it could defeat the conjecture on this page by
      giving the infinities a genuine common clock. Merely drawing the two endpoints next to one another, or
      reflecting \(u\mapsto-u\), does not do that. Read
      <a href="/inverted-toruses/#t-not-time">why handedness reversal is not time reversal</a>.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The Temporal-Antipode Test</p>
    <h2 id="t-test">What Must Be Derived Before “Cannot” Means Physics</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The conjecture is presently metaphysical structure. To enter physics it must expose
      a finite observable and give that observable permission to disagree.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Derive the state</h3>
      <p>Name the degrees of freedom whose phases are being compared. &ldquo;Infinity&rdquo; is not itself a clock,
        field or Hilbert-space state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Derive the transport</h3>
      <p>Specify how both endpoint projections are carried to one finite comparison surface without assuming
        the synchrony or asynchrony the calculation is meant to test.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Freeze a synchronisation error</h3>
      <p>Before looking at data, define a dimensionless interval statistic such as</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathcal S_I=\inf_{k\in\mathbb Z}
        \left[\sup_{t\in I}|\Delta\phi-2\pi k|
        +T_I\sup_{t\in I}|\Delta\omega|\right].
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Allow the clean loss</h3>
      <p>The conjecture fails if a lawful model yields \(\mathcal S_I=0\) on a non-zero interval, if a
        physical temporal gluing provides a shared clock, or if every observed mismatch can be rescued with
        an object-specific phase offset.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="muted small">Ordinary propagation delays, redshift, decoherence and unsynchronised astrophysical
    sources are the null explanations. They are not evidence for temporal antipodes merely because they also
    produce lag.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Read the diagram without mistaking it for a clock</p>
    <h2 id="t-map-versus-time">A Finite Page Can Show Infinity Without Bringing It Nearby</h2>
    <p>A world map can place the North Pole at the top edge of a rectangle, but the printed centimetres do not
      tell a traveller how long the journey will take. Compactification works in a similar way. A function such
      as \(\tau=(2/\pi)\arctan t\) maps every finite time \(t\) into the interval \((-1,1)\). As \(t\) grows
      without bound, τ approaches 1 but never reaches it at any finite time.</p>
    <p>The drawing is useful because an infinite history now fits on a finite page. It preserves ordering: later
      finite times still appear later. It does not preserve ordinary durations, because equal steps in τ
      represent larger and larger steps in \(t\) near the edge. Nor does the top boundary become a physical
      event merely because the diagram includes it.</p>
    <p>TTI assigns smallward and largeward infinities to opposite compactified ends. This is a clear geometric
      proposal: finite history lies between two asymptotic conditions. The additional claim that those ends
      cannot synchronise is conditional. It follows only if no lawful identification or transport rule joins
      them. Topological separation on one diagram is not by itself a prohibition in physics.</p>
    <p class="lede">Compactification changes coordinates, not causal structure. A boundary drawn at a finite
      distance can still represent something no finite traveller reaches.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What synchronisation requires</p>
    <h2 id="t-clock-protocol">Two Clocks Agree Only After a Comparison Rule Is Chosen</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A shared event</h3>
      <p>Two clocks brought together can compare readings locally. Separated clocks need exchanged signals and
        a convention for propagation delay. In relativity, simultaneity for distant events depends on the
        observer&rsquo;s state of motion; there is no universal cosmic tick supplied automatically.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A stable rate relation</h3>
      <p>Matching once is not synchronisation. The rates must remain related over an interval after known
        redshift, motion and gravitational effects are included. Two hands that cross on a clock face may have
        different speeds and immediately separate.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A transported phase</h3>
      <p>Phase belongs to a specified oscillator. Comparing phases at different places requires a signal or a
        transport law. Without one, drawing two sine waves on the same axis silently grants the common time
        coordinate that the antipode proposal says may be absent.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>An error tolerance</h3>
      <p>Real clocks have noise. An operational claim defines how much phase and rate disagreement counts as
        failure, over what duration, and against which null models. The synchronisation functional above is a
        template for making “never” answerable by data.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">From a philosophical picture to a risky statement</p>
    <h2 id="t-antipode-observables">What Evidence Could Bear on Temporal Antipodes?</h2>
    <p>The endpoints themselves are asymptotic, so they cannot be placed side by side in a laboratory. Evidence
      would have to come through finite projections. A model must specify a field or signal carrying one
      endpoint&rsquo;s influence, derive how it propagates through finite spacetime, and predict a pattern that is
      not freely adjustable for every source.</p>
    <p>A universal phase drift, for example, would need the same functional dependence across independent
      systems after ordinary cosmological redshift and local dynamics were removed. A single lag is weak
      evidence because distance, scattering, source variability and instrument calibration already create
      lags. The value would lie in a constrained relation predicted before the observations were opened.</p>
    <p>The proposal can also lose without direct access to infinity. If a complete physical model supplies a
      lawful shared clock and gives zero synchronisation error over a non-zero interval, the strong
      “cannot synchronise” claim fails. If every supposed signature needs a new source-specific phase, the
      proposal explains no more than unconstrained curve fitting. If compactification alone produces no
      observable, it remains a philosophical boundary picture rather than a physical theory.</p>
    <p>None of this removes the idea&rsquo;s philosophical force. Two unreachable limits can orient a finite story
      without becoming moments inside it. The discipline is to keep that interpretive role visible while
      withholding physical certainty until a carrier, transport law and risky prediction exist.</p>
    <p>An important guardrail comes from relativity. Observers following different paths can accumulate
      different proper times and still compare clocks consistently when they meet. That difference is not a
      failure of time itself to synchronise; it is a predicted consequence of motion and gravity. Any antipode
      signal must be distinguishable from ordinary time dilation by using the relativistic prediction as its
      null model.</p>
    <p>Likewise, cosmological redshift stretches observed periods as the universe expands. Source evolution and
      propagation through matter can add further delays. A claimed residual should survive correction for
      these effects with one common law. Otherwise the framework would be using known disagreements between
      clocks as evidence for a stronger proposition they do not imply.</p>
    <p>This is why the conjecture is most defensible as a boundary hypothesis. It proposes an orientation for
      finite history and invites a future theory to transmit that structure inward. Until then, it neither
      overrules relativity nor supplies an absolute present. The limit points guide the model; local clocks
      remain governed by tested spacetime physics.</p>
    <p class="muted">Earned mathematics: compactification and clock-comparison principles. Chosen structure:
      opposite temporal assignment. Open research: whether finite observables carry a distinctive trace of
      that assignment. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

